{# ==========================================================================
     05 · Speed
     The two charts and the tables under them are measured, from
     github.com/phplrt/benchmarks — absolute values, in µs, ms and kb.

     The "how much work do you need?" panel is deliberately NOT absolute. It
     shows three percentages of a full parse, and its caveat box says so. If
     microseconds ever go back in there, the caveat has to go back too.
     ====================================================================== #}
<section class="band band--sunk" id="speed">
    <div class="hold">
        <div class="head rise">
            <span class="chapter">05 · speed</span>
            <h2 class="title">Ten times the input, ten times the time</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                On a single short type every parser here takes microseconds, and on the
                shortest of them the hand-written ones win: <code>phpstan/phpdoc-parser</code>
                reads <code>string|int</code> in 4.9 µs, phplrt in 11. From a kilobyte on
                phplrt is the fastest of the five, and the gap keeps opening: phplrt takes
                ×9.6 longer for every ×10 more input, which is about as close to linear as
                parsing gets, while <code>phpstan/phpdoc-parser</code> takes ×24 longer.
                On 10 KB that is 5.1 ms against 14.7 ms.
            </p>
        </div>

        {#
          COORDINATE PLAN — shared by both charts on this page.

          Grid (1000 × 500). Plot box x 96…800, y 30…430.

            x = 96 + (log10(bytes) − 1) × 233.87
                10 B  = 96      64 B = 284.5    256 B = 425.3
                1 KB  = 566.1   4 KB = 706.9    10 KB = 800

          The 6 samples span three decades (10 B → 10 KB), so the axis is a
          true log axis and the ticks land where the data does. 64 B → 4 KB
          are evenly spaced because each is ×4 the one before; the last two
          sit closer because 4 → 10 KB really is only ×2.5.

          Time, five decades over 400px (1 µs … 100 ms):
            y = 430 − (log10(ms) + 3) × 80
                10 µs = 350   100 µs = 270   1 ms = 190   10 ms = 110   100 ms = 30

          Memory, four decades over 400px (1 kb … 10 000 kb, 1 kb = 1024 bytes):
            y = 430 − log10(kb) × 100
                10 kb = 330   100 kb = 230   1 000 kb = 130

          Every point below is that formula applied to the table in the same
          panel; nothing here is eyeballed.
        #}
